Theory :

A Binary Tree is a non-linear data structure in which each node can have at most two children, known as the left child and the right child. The topmost node is called the root, while nodes that do not have any children are called leaf nodes. Binary trees are widely used to represent hierarchical relationships and organize data efficiently.
Tree traversal is the process of visiting every node in a binary tree in a specific order. The three common depth-first traversal techniques are Inorder, Preorder, and
Postorder traversal.

In Inorder Traversal, nodes are visited in the order Left, Root, Right. In Preorder Traversal, nodes are visited in the order Root, Left, Right. In Postorder Traversal, nodes are visited in the order Left, Right, Root. These techniques are useful for processing tree data in different sequences.
Binary trees have several real-world applications. For example, they can represent decision-making processes, file-system hierarchies, and expression evaluation in mathematical calculations. In a binary search tree, data can also be organized to support efficient searching, insertion, and deletion.





